    "https://jobs.jmu.edu/jobs/assistant-professor-of-english-linguistics-tenure-track-harrisonburg-virginia-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of English: Linguistics",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of English: Linguistics - Tenure Track - Harrisonburg, Virginia, United States Home Explore JMU Benefits Navigate JMU Job Search My Profile Applicant Login Kiosk mode Assistant Professor of English: Linguistics - Tenure Track 20002312 College of Arts and Letters Instructional / Teaching Faculty Instructional / Teaching Faculty Opening on: Sep 22 2026 Academic Affairs 100020 - English Department Add to favorites View favorites Working Title: Assistant Professor of English: Linguistics - Tenure Track State Role Title: N/A Position Type: Instructional / Teaching Faculty Position Status: Full-Time FLSA Status: Exempt: Not Eligible for Overtime College/Division: College of Arts and Letters Department: 100020 - English Department Pay Band: N/A Pay Rate: Commensurate with Experience Specify Range or Amount: Is this a JMU only position? No Is this a grant-funded position? No Is this a Conflict of Interest designated position? No Beginning Review Date: 11/01/2026 About JMU: Mission We are a community committed to preparing students to be educated and enlightened citizens who lead productive and meaningful lives. Vision To be the national model for the engaged university: engaged with ideas and the world. Who We Are Situated in the heart of Virginia’s beautiful Shenandoah Valley, the city of Harrisonburg is a vibrant community with a population of approximately 52,000. Harrisonburg is conveniently located approximately 120 miles from Washington, D.C. and Richmond, VA. JMU is a selective, public institution with a growing national reputation for offering experiences that lead to an outstanding education and supportive environment for students, faculty and staff. The student body includes approximately 21,000 undergraduate and 1,800 graduate students, with over 1,000 full-time instructional faculty. JMU offers thriving programs in the liberal arts, science and technology, and professional disciplines at the undergraduate, master’s and doctoral levels. JMU has achieved national recognition for the high quality of its academic programs, focus on maintaining strong student/faculty interaction, and innovative faculty research. General Information: The Department of English at James Madison University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professorship in Linguistics to begin August 2027. The Department offers a B.A. and an M.A. English and serves students in the university’s General Education program. It houses the University’s thriving creative writing minor, and faculty teach across many interdisciplinary minors, including African, African American and Diaspora Studies; American Studies; Digital Studies; Disability Studies; Environmental Humanities; Film Studies; Latin American, Latinx, and Caribbean Studies; Linguistics; Medieval and Renaissance Studies; Queer Studies; Women’s, Gender and Sexuality Studies; and World Literature. The College of Arts and Letters, in which the Department of English is located, enrolls over 3100 undergraduate majors and about 150 graduate students across ten academic units in the humanities, social sciences, and communication studies. Home to several interdisciplinary centers and institutes, the College employs 270 full-time faculty. We embrace the teacher-scholar model, and encourage excellent applicants within our particular disciplines, as well as scholars and teachers eager to work across them. Duties and Responsibilities: The successful candidate will be expected to teach introductory, advanced, and graduate (M.A.) courses in Linguistics to English majors and minors, Pre-Professional Secondary Education (SEED) majors and minors, Linguistics minors, and contribute to the university’s General Education Program. This hire will also contribute to the English curriculum by offering literature courses for majors and minors and in the General Education program. Qualifications: Required: •PhD in Linguistics or a related field by August 31, 2027 •Evidence of excellence or potential for excellence in teaching in Linguistics and Literature •Evidence of excellence or potential for excellence in publishing in an area of Linguistics •Evidence of excellence or potential for excellence in departmental, university, or professional service in support of the fields of Linguistics and English Additional Considerations: •We welcome applications from candidates with secondary interest and experience in one or more of the interdisciplinary minors in the College of Arts and Letters. •We welcome applications from candidates with secondary interest and ability in teaching Histories of English. • We seek candidates with a demonstrated commitment to building a supportive, welcoming scholarly environment. Additional Posting Information: Interested candidate will complete the online application and provide: •Curriculum Vitae •Statement of Teaching Philosophy •Cover Letter •Professional Writing Sample •Names and contact information for three references JMU’s Dual Career and Community Resources Program is available for candidates that will be relocating to the area. For more information, please visit https://bit.ly/2OTF1NG. Conditions of Employment: Employment is contingent upon the successful completion of a criminal background check. E-Verify Notice: After accepting employment, new hires are required to complete an I-9 form and present documentation of their identity and eligibility to work in the United States. James Madison University uses the E-Verify system to confirm identity and work authorization. EEO Statement: James Madison University is committed to creating and supporting a diverse and inclusive work and educational community that is free of all forms of discrimination. This institution does not tolerate discrimination or harassment on the basis of age, color, disability, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, parental status, political affiliation, race, religion, sex, sexual orientation or veteran status. We promote access, inclusion and diversity for all students, faculty, staff, constituents and programs, believing that these qualities are foundational components of an outstanding education in keeping with our mission. The university is interested in candidates whose experience and qualifications support an ongoing commitment to this core quality. Anyone having questions concerning discrimination should contact the Office for Equal Opportunity: (540) 568-6991. Reasonable Accommodation: If you are an individual with a disability and need assistance searching or applying for jobs please contact us at (540) 568-3597 or jobs@jmu.edu. You may also visit the JMU Human Resource Office, located at 752 Ott Street, Harrisonburg, VA 22807 and we will be happy to assist you. Apply Now Total Compensation Estimator Sign Up for Job Alerts! First Name (required) 32c6a1fb Last Name (required) dc704b76 Email (required) 339bc72c Job Alert ee1b4e74 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories Instructional / Teaching Faculty Instructional / Teaching Faculty Employment Type Instructional / Teaching Faculty Instructional / Teaching Faculty Locations Harrisonburg, Virginia, United States Harrisonburg, Virginia, United States Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Know someone who would be interested in this job? First Name (required) 02524f67 Last Name (required) de008b65 Email (required) 8774cef2 Who would you like to send this job to? b3f95a7d Refer Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Recently Posted Jobs Associate Director, Research and Public Health 20002267 Instructional / Teaching Faculty Administrative & Professional (Non-teaching Faculty) The Institute for Innovation in Health and Human Services (IIHHS) is accepting applications for a full-time Associate Director of Research and Public Health.\n\nThe Associate Director serves on the IIHHS Leadership Team, and takes a lead role in IIHHS strategic initiatives related to research and evaluation. This position also supervises and contributes to public health evaluation efforts done in partnership with the State, and is responsible for collaborating on grants to continue the public health research activities conducted out of IIHHS. This position also assists in grant applications to continue and grow our work across the Institute, and particularly in the areas of Research and Public Health. This role includes mentoring faculty and students as collaborators on research projects, and supports in-house evaluation efforts to build program capacity for evaluation. The Associate Director is a thought partner for and leads efforts to document social impact of IIHHS. The Associate Director supports the annual reporting efforts of IIHHS, including helping to strengthen and disseminate the annual report survey, preparing the data for the Director to include in the written annual report, and supporting data visualization efforts.\n\nIIHHS is seeking a creative, self-starting team member, who is effective working alone and as part of a team. The Associate Director supervises and works collaboratively with the PT Operations Coordinator, and also works collaboratively with colleagues across the Institute. The Associate Director ensures that all fiscal and personnel functions of the department operate at optimum efficiency, that grant funding supports program operations, and that deliverables stay on schedule and within budget. The Associate Director reports to the Director of IIHHS and provides fiscal and administrative support for areas and positions within the department.\n\nThis is not a remote/telework position. Student Case Administrator - Restorative Practices - IE 20002325 Student Employment Institutional Employment Student Case Administrators (SCAs) are undergraduate students who conduct one-