    "https://jobs.jmu.edu/jobs/academic-success-communications-content-assistant-ie-harrisonburg-virginia-united-states": {
      "title": "Academic Success Communications Content Assistant",
      "description": "Academic Success Communications Content Assistant - IE - Harrisonburg, Virginia, United States Home Explore JMU Benefits Navigate JMU Job Search My Profile Applicant Login Kiosk mode Academic Success Communications Content Assistant - IE 20002310 Student Academic Success Student Employment Institutional Employment Opening on: Sep 22 2026 Closing on: Oct 9 2026 Academic Affairs 100540 - Academic Student Services Add to favorites View favorites Working Title: Academic Success Communications Content Assistant - IE State Role Title: N/A Position Type: Institutional Employment Position Status: Part-Time FLSA Status: Non-Exempt: Eligible for Overtime College/Division: Student Academic Success Department: 100540 - Academic Student Services Pay Band: Pay Rate: Hourly Specify Range or Amount: $13-$15 Is this a JMU only position? No Is this a grant-funded position? No Is this a Conflict of Interest designated position? N/A Beginning Review Date: About JMU: Situated in the heart of Virginia’s beautiful Shenandoah Valley, the city of Harrisonburg is a vibrant community with a population of approximately 52,000. Harrisonburg is conveniently located approximately 120 miles from Washington, D.C. and Richmond, VA. JMU is a selective, public institution with a growing national reputation for offering experiences that lead to an outstanding education and supportive environment for students, faculty and staff. The student body includes approximately 21,000 undergraduate and 1,800 graduate students, with over 1,000 full-time instructional faculty. JMU offers thriving programs in the liberal arts, science and technology, and professional disciplines at the undergraduate, master’s and doctoral levels. JMU has achieved national recognition for the high quality of its academic programs, focus on maintaining strong student/faculty interaction, and innovative faculty research. General Information: Student Academic Success (SAS) seeks a detail-oriented student writer to research and write stories for the unit’s academic success magazine and online newsletter. Get paid to tell great stories. Interview students, alumni, donors, faculty, and staff while gaining real-world experience and building a strong writing portfolio. The Academic Success Communications Content Assistant must be available to work 10 hours per week. Duties and Responsibilities: • Research, write, and edit engaging academic success stories for print and digital publications. • Conduct interviews with students, alumni, donors, faculty, and staff. • Work with the Student Academic Success Communications team to create story timelines that ensure content deadlines are met. • Collaborate with the Student Academic Success Communications team and Student Academic Success units on edits and feedback. • Work both collaboratively and independently, and meet project deadlines. • Get trained and build articles in Cascade (JMU web management system). Qualifications: • Current degree-seeking student, enrolled on at least a half-time basis (at least 6 credit hours) • Strong writing and editing skills • Ability to conduct oneself professionally when interacting with interview subjects. • Detail-oriented with good time management and prioritization skills • Working knowledge or interest in journalism, marketing, public relations, or storytelling • Knowledge or experience in the following is helpful, but not required: web-based management system, Microsoft Office • Skills in graphic design, videography, and/or photography are a plus Additional Posting Information: To apply: Submit the Student application and upload your resume and one writing sample (including essays). Conditions of Employment: Students must be degree-seeking students, enrolled on at least a half-time basis (6 credits for undergraduate and 5 credits for graduate). E-Verify Notice: After accepting employment, new hires are required to complete an I-9 form and present documentation of their identity and eligibility to work in the United States. James Madison University uses the E-Verify system to confirm identity and work authorization. EEO Statement: James Madison University is committed to creating and supporting a diverse and inclusive work and educational community that is free of all forms of discrimination. This institution does not tolerate discrimination or harassment on the basis of age, color, disability, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, parental status, political affiliation, race, religion, sex, sexual orientation or veteran status. We promote access, inclusion and diversity for all students, faculty, staff, constituents and programs, believing that these qualities are foundational components of an outstanding education in keeping with our mission. The university is interested in candidates whose experience and qualifications support an ongoing commitment to this core quality. Anyone having questions concerning discrimination should contact the Office for Equal Opportunity: (540) 568-6991. Reasonable Accommodation: If you are a student with a disability and need assistance please contact the Office of Disability Services at (540) 568-6705 or disability-svcs@jmu.edu. You may also visit the Office of Disability Services, located in the Student Success Center, Suite 1202 and they will be happy to assist you. Apply Now Total Compensation Estimator Recently Posted Jobs Associate Director, Research and Public Health 20002267 Instructional / Teaching Faculty Administrative & Professional (Non-teaching Faculty) The Institute for Innovation in Health and Human Services (IIHHS) is accepting applications for a full-time Associate Director of Research and Public Health.\n\nThe Associate Director serves on the IIHHS Leadership Team, and takes a lead role in IIHHS strategic initiatives related to research and evaluation. This position also supervises and contributes to public health evaluation efforts done in partnership with the State, and is responsible for collaborating on grants to continue the public health research activities conducted out of IIHHS. This position also assists in grant applications to continue and grow our work across the Institute, and particularly in the areas of Research and Public Health. This role includes mentoring faculty and students as collaborators on research projects, and supports in-house evaluation efforts to build program capacity for evaluation. The Associate Director is a thought partner for and leads efforts to document social impact of IIHHS. The Associate Director supports the annual reporting efforts of IIHHS, including helping to strengthen and disseminate the annual report survey, preparing the data for the Director to include in the written annual report, and supporting data visualization efforts.\n\nIIHHS is seeking a creative, self-starting team member, who is effective working alone and as part of a team. The Associate Director supervises and works collaboratively with the PT Operations Coordinator, and also works collaboratively with colleagues across the Institute. The Associate Director ensures that all fiscal and personnel functions of the department operate at optimum efficiency, that grant funding supports program operations, and that deliverables stay on schedule and within budget. The Associate Director reports to the Director of IIHHS and provides fiscal and administrative support for areas and positions within the department.\n\nThis is not a remote/telework position. Student Case Administrator - Restorative Practices - IE 20002325 Student Employment Institutional Employment Student Case Administrators (SCAs) are undergraduate students who conduct one-on-one Individual Accountability Case Reviews for students who are alleged to have violated university policy. In Individual Accountability Case Reviews, SCAs evaluate evidence to determine whether or not the Responding Party is responsible for violating policy and, if applicable, assign outcomes. Student Case Administrators are undergraduate staff members in the Office of Student Accountability and Restorative Practices who participate in staff training and professional development; assist with various programs, outreach opportunities, and educational programs; and collaborate in the recruitment and selection of staff and volunteers. Physician Assistant Studies Graduate Program Director 20002311 Instructional / Teaching Faculty Instructional / Teaching Faculty The Department of Health Professions in the College of Health and Behavioral Studies (CHBS) at James Madison University invites applications for Graduate Program Director of the Physician Assistant Studies program at the rank of Associate or Full Professor of Practice. This is a renewable term appointment. Accredited by ARC-PA, the program boasts a 98% first-time PANCE pass rate over the past five years and is supported by a stable, experienced faculty. The position offers opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration across Health Professions programs and throughout the College. The preferred start date is January 1, 2027, with some flexibility based on candidate availability and institutional needs.\n\nThe successful candidate will join an engaged faculty at one of the nation's leading public universities. CHBS is committed to fostering a welcoming, innovative environment that supports individual and collective growth through education, scholarship, and service. Learn more about CHBS at https://www.jmu.edu/chbs/about.shtml Privacy Policy Quick Links Accessibility at JMU Application Instructions Notice of Availability - Annual Security and Fire Safety Report Perm Notices Student Employment Employment Posters © 2026 JAMES MADISON UNIVERSITY Close Candidate Details We use cookies. Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalize, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
    },
