    "https://jobs.jmu.edu/jobs/communications-content-assistant-student-fws-harrisonburg-virginia-united-states": {
      "title": "Communications Content Assistant",
      "description": "Communications Content Assistant - Student FWS - Harrisonburg, Virginia, United States Home Explore JMU Benefits Navigate JMU Job Search My Profile Applicant Login Kiosk mode Communications Content Assistant - Student FWS 20002236 Advancement Marketing Student Employment Federal Work Study Opening on: Aug 26 2026 Philanthropy and Engagement 100445 - Philanthropic Communications Add to favorites View favorites Working Title: Communications Content Assistant - FWS State Role Title: Communications Content Assistant Position Type: Federal Work Study Position Status: Part-Time FLSA Status: N/A College/Division: Advancement Marketing Department: 100445 - Philanthropic Communications Pay Band: Pay Rate: Hourly Specify Range or Amount: $13-15 Is this a JMU only position? No Is this a grant-funded position? N/A Is this a Conflict of Interest designated position? N/A Beginning Review Date: 09/14/2026 About JMU: General Information: Kickstart your storytelling career while you’re taking classes at JMU! JMU’s Philanthropic Communications team is looking for a curious, detail-oriented Communications Content Assistant to join our collaborative, fast-paced environment. In this role, you’ll research and/or be assigned to write stories that appear across campus and to our alumni and donors. While sharpening your journalistic voice and building a strong writing portfolio, you’ll be paid to interview students, faculty and alumni, cover events and build web content. If you love a good story, write cleanly under AP style and want hands-on experience with professional marketing and fundraising communications, this is your opportunity! Hours: - This position is 10-12 hours per week. - Hours must be worked between 8 a.m. and 5 p.m. Monday through Friday, during the semester. (We will determine your work schedule based on your availability.) - Provided you continue to remain eligible for Federal Work Study, it may be possible for you to retain this position for future fall and/or spring semesters. Work Location: Sonner Hall (next to College of Business) Duties and Responsibilities: - Research, write, and edit stories related to giving to the university for web and print using AP style and JMU’s Editorial Style Guide - Conduct interviews with donors, alumni, faculty, and students to gather quotes for stories - Support the Philanthropic Communications team with content creation leading up to events - Attend our philanthropy events (such as Giving Day, Bluestone Reunion and Women for Madison) to gather story content - Build and update articles and pages using JMU’s web-based CMS (Cascade) - Collaborate with the Philanthropic Communications team and the departments we serve on edits and feedback - Work both collaboratively and independently, and meet project deadlines - Develop future content with audience-informed work Qualifications: - You must be a current JMU student (enrolled in at least 6 credits) who qualifies for Federal Work Study. Verification of FWS eligibility must be provided with your application. You can log on to your MyMadison and under Finances click ‘View My Financial Aid.’ Click on the current Aid Year. Copy and paste your award information into a Word document, and save. This document must be sent electronically along with your application when applying for FWS jobs. As soon as you have verification of your FWS eligibility, you can begin applying for FWS positions. - Strong writing and editing skills, with familiarity in or willingness to learn AP style - Comfortable interviewing subjects and attending events to gather content - Detail-oriented with good time management and prioritization skills - Working knowledge or interest in journalism, marketing, public relations, or storytelling - Knowledge or experience in the following is also helpful, but not required: web-based CMS (we use Cascade), Microsoft Office - Skills in graphic design, videography, or photography are a plus Additional Posting Information: The Right Candidate Will: - Bring a can-do attitude and a willingness to jump in, brainstorm and bounce ideas around with the team - Value a diversity of ideas and perspectives, and bring an open mind to feedback - Be a people person who enjoys connecting with donors, alumni and colleagues across campus - Be systems-oriented, with a knack for staying organized and following a process through to completion To Apply: upload your resume, 1-2 writing samples (can include essays), and FWS eligibility. Conditions of Employment: Students must be degree-seeking students, enrolled on at least a half-time basis (6 credits for undergraduate and 5 credits for graduate). E-Verify Notice: After accepting employment, new hires are required to complete an I-9 form and present documentation of their identity and eligibility to work in the United States. James Madison University uses the E-Verify system to confirm identity and work authorization. EEO Statement: Reasonable Accommodation: If you are a student with a disability and need assistance please contact the Office of Disability Services at (540) 568-6705 or disability-svcs@jmu.edu. You may also visit the Office of Disability Services, located in the Student Success Center, Suite 1202 and they will be happy to assist you Apply Now Total Compensation Estimator Sign Up for Job Alerts! First Name (required) f8e8a1c9 Last Name (required) 52283599 Email (required) dda30f54 Job Alert 78514be1 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories Student Employment Student Employment Employment Type Federal Work Study Federal Work Study Locations Harrisonburg, Virginia, United States Harrisonburg, Virginia, United States Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Know someone who would be interested in this job? First Name (required) 07847d87 Last Name (required) 8d5a3117 Email (required) 71f7a851 Who would you like to send this job to? 4b880da6 Refer Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Recently Posted Jobs Associate Director, Research and Public Health 20002267 Instructional / Teaching Faculty Administrative & Professional (Non-teaching Faculty) The Institute for Innovation in Health and Human Services (IIHHS) is accepting applications for a full-time Associate Director of Research and Public Health.\n\nThe Associate Director serves on the IIHHS Leadership Team, and takes a lead role in IIHHS strategic initiatives related to research and evaluation. This position also supervises and contributes to public health evaluation efforts done in partnership with the State, and is responsible for collaborating on grants to continue the public health research activities conducted out of IIHHS. This position also assists in grant applications to continue and grow our work across the Institute, and particularly in the areas of Research and Public Health. This role includes mentoring faculty and students as collaborators on research projects, and supports in-house evaluation efforts to build program capacity for evaluation. The Associate Director is a thought partner for and leads efforts to document social impact of IIHHS. The Associate Director supports the annual reporting efforts of IIHHS, including helping to strengthen and disseminate the annual report survey, preparing the data for the Director to include in the written annual report, and supporting data visualization efforts.\n\nIIHHS is seeking a creative, self-starting team member, who is effective working alone and as part of a team. The Associate Director supervises and works collaboratively with the PT Operations Coordinator, and also works collaboratively with colleagues across the Institute. The Associate Director ensures that all fiscal and personnel functions of the department operate at optimum efficiency, that grant funding supports program operations, and that deliverables stay on schedule and within budget. The Associate Director reports to the Director of IIHHS and provides fiscal and administrative support for areas and positions within the department.\n\nThis is not a remote/telework position. Student Case Administrator - Restorative Practices - IE 20002325 Student Employment Institutional Employment Student Case Administrators (SCAs) are undergraduate students who conduct one-on-one Individual Accountability Case Reviews for students who are alleged to have violated university policy. In Individual Accountability Case Reviews, SCAs evaluate evidence to determine whether or not the Responding Party is responsible for violating policy and, if applicable, assign outcomes. Student Case Administrators are undergraduate staff members in the Office of Student Accountability and Restorative Practices who participate in staff training and professional development; assist with various programs, outreach opportunities, and educational programs; and collaborate in the recruitment and selection of staff and volunteers. Physician Assistant Studies Graduate Program Director 20002311 Instructional / Teaching Faculty Instructional / Teaching Faculty The Department of Health Professions in the College of Health and Behavioral Studies (CHBS) at James Madison University invites applications for Graduate Program Director of the Physician Assistant Studies program at the rank of Associate or Full Professor of Practice. This is a renewable term appointment. Accredited by ARC-PA, the program boasts a 98% first-time PANCE pass rate over the past five years and is supported by a stable, experienced faculty. The position offers opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration across Health Professions programs and throughout the College. The preferred start date is January 1, 2027, with some flexibility based on candidate availability and institutional needs.\n\nThe successful candidate will join an engaged faculty at one of the nation's leading public universities. CHBS is committed to fostering a welcoming, innovative environment that supports individual and collective growth through education, scholarship, and service. Learn more about CHBS at https://www.jmu.edu/chbs/about.shtml Apply Now First Name (required) 005b6e91 Last Name (required) ac1a5c69 Email (required) 9d790b42 Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Privacy Policy Quick Links Accessibility at JMU Application Instructions Notice of Availability - Annual Security and Fire Safety Report Perm Notices Student Employment Employment Posters © 2026 JAMES MADISON UNIVERSITY Before You Go.. Would You Like To Sign Up For Job Alerts? First Name (required) 53cfc91d Last Name (required) 9a1144f9 Email (required) 12ce4630 Job Alert 5be22963 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories Student Employment Student Employment Employment Type Federal Work Study Federal Work Study Locations Harrisonburg, Virginia, United States Harrisonburg, Virginia, United States Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Close Candidate Details We use cookies. Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalize, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all Please choose the location(s)"
